  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Find the dimensions of a rectangle whose area is $6\\text{ m}^2$ and whose perimeter is $11\\text{ m}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\frac32\\text{ m}$ by $4\\text{ m}$.\n\nUse the perimeter to express one side in terms of the other, then factorise the area equation.",
      "worked": "Let the dimensions be $x$ metres and $y$ metres.\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+2y&=11\\\\x+y&=\\frac{11}{2}\\\\y&=\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the area:\n$$\\begin{align*}x\\left(\\frac{11}{2}-x\\right)&=6\\\\11x-2x^2&=12\\\\2x^2-11x+12&=0\\\\(2x-3)(x-4)&=0\\\\x&=\\frac32\\text{ or }4\\end{align*}$$\nThe corresponding other side is $4$ or $\\frac32$ metres. Thus the dimensions are $\\frac32\\text{ m}$ by $4\\text{ m}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 106
  },
